              "Yes. The review identifies three additional simplifications:\n\n- **One local stationary curve.** Immediately before any finite collision, the entire local stationary set is the parity-matched predecessor curve. The opposite branch is absent there.\n- **One vanishing scalar.** Along that curve, \\(W=h_j\\) generates both physical jet coordinates, and\n  \\[\n  E=W^2\\bigl(1+O(s^2)\\bigr),\\qquad s=T-t.\n  \\]\n  Fixed positive cones of starting jets retain every collision predecessor eventually. On these cones, the boundary target reduces to\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{W B_{\\partial}\\le C W^2+\\varepsilon|W|.}\n  \\]\n- **One arithmetic boundary invariant.** The source and index boundary contributions combine into a single scalar trace. A gauge can move the contribution between boundaries, but cannot improve its sign.\n\nI recommend using this scalar formulation for the next arithmetic investigation. It also permits weaker reconstruction-coefficient bounds than optimization over the full jet plane. The remaining task is still a source-derived bound on the unfavorable part of the trace, with uniform errors.\n\nSaved the [full derivation](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-2/active/22_theta_manifold_geometry/notes/01_physical_jet_geometry/03_SCALAR_STATIONARY_GEOMETRY_AND_INVARIANT_TRACE_20261015.md) and [independent internal review](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-2/active/22_theta_manifold_geometry/reviews/11_SCALAR_STATIONARY_GEOMETRY_REVIEW_20261015.md). Supporting finite identities passed **1,291 exact rational checks**, independently replayed. The manuscript and existing notes remain unchanged."
            ]
